// A three-component vector, generic over the unit its components carry.
//
// One declaration serves every quantity. `Vec3(m)` is a position, `Vec3(m/s)` a
// velocity, `Vec3(m/s^2)` a specific force, `Vec3(rad/s)` an angular rate --
// four types in the predecessor C++ (`SAELinPos`, `SAELinVel`, `SAELinAccel`,
// `SAEAngVel`), identical in shape and distinguished only by the unit of their
// fields. That is exactly what a unit parameter is for (§5.2, §6.2a).
//
// The binding is per REFERENCE, not per instance, because a type is never
// instantiated. Two ports that bound `U` COMPATIBLY convert, field by field and
// exactly as a scalar port does: `Vec3(mm)` into `Vec3(m)` folds a factor of
// 1/1000 into each field at elaboration (§5.2). Two that bound it to different
// DIMENSIONS are `SE0410` -- the same error a scalar `(m)` into `(m/s)` gets,
// reported once against the argument rather than once per field.
//
// COORDINATE CONVENTION -- SAE, right-handed, as the field comments state. This
// is documentation and nothing else: the compiler does not model frames, and it
// cannot. A unit conversion folds to a compile-time constant (§4.2), but a
// body-to-inertial transform needs the current attitude, which is a run-time
// value. So a frame could only ever be CHECKED, never converted, and that check
// does not exist. Mixing a body vector with an inertial one is a modelling
// error this type will not catch for you.
//
// Fields are `double` deliberately. `SE0431` permits only `double` as a
// `continuous` state, so should §5.2's records-as-states restriction ever lift,
// nothing here forecloses it.
package se.math;

type Vec3 {
    units { U; }

    x: double(U);   // points forward for a body frame, north for a global one
    y: double(U);   // points right for a body frame, east for a global one
    z: double(U);   // points down, in both
}
